This is a good point, and I agree to some extent. Certainly there is a
lot more at stake in the late game. I'm glad that you can't just fall
into a pit and die instantly from poison with the amulet! And I
appreciate your point about being given freedom to play around a bit
with different spells and weapons. I sometimes refer to the last part
of the game (returning with the amulet) as a 'victory lap'. But never
the less, I think the easiness goes too far. And sometimes I find the
end-game to be a bit tedious rather than fun. (particularly the plane
of fire... watching a bazillion attacks get resisted every step really
spoils that place for me, but that's another story.)  I guess there is
a trade-off between the things we are talking about, and perhaps you
like it the way it is, but I'd prefer to shift the balance a bit.


