> I remember an idea someone came up with years ago that I thought was
> pretty good: make the fire and cold resistance intrinsics mutually
> exclusive. That is, you can either be fire resistant (intrinsically)
> or cold resistant (intrinsically), but not both. Getting one removes
> the other. But you can still get the other resistance extrinsically.
> This makes red or blue dragon scale mail a valid choice, and makes
> fire/cold resistance rings considerably more valuable.


Interesting thoughts about resistances. I would like the other dragon
scales (ie. non-grey/silver) to see a bit more action, and these kinds
of idea to fit with my objection to being "immune to everything". But
I'm not sure if making fire-res and cold-res mutual exclusive is the
right way to do it. Another possibility is to make all of the
intrinsic resistance be temporary. For example, maybe eating a white
dragon would give cold resistance for 4000-8000 turns, or something
like that. With a system like that, resistance rings would be useful
throughout the game; and it would also become useful to carry around
tins of dragon meat... But on the other hand, maybe it would be a bit
too fiddly =96 ie. the extra micro-management might be unfun. Also,
monks would be relatively much more powerful than they are now. I
think I'll just leave all the resistances alone for the time being,
and maybe revisit these ideas when I'm looking for more changes to
play with. :p


